== §6.1 Bloom filters: the two procedures
OK   BloomMembership never answers False for a member (no false negatives), and answers False exactly when some probed bit is 0 (300 random filters)
OK   figure example: cat, dog, emu set bits 0, 1, 3, 6, 9, 11, 14 (7 of 16; bits 6 and 11 are set twice)
OK   figure example: fox (2, 9, 14) is rejected by bit 2; gnu (1, 9, 14) is a false positive
OK   figure example: a query with fresh random hashes is a false positive with probability (7/16)^3 = 343/4096 = 8.4%
== §6.2 False positive rate
OK   Pr[B[h] = 0] = (1 - 1/m)^{kn} and the exact false-positive rate, by enumerating every outcome (8 tiny cases)
OK   footnote 3: (1 - e^{-kn/m})^k < (1 - (1-1/m)^{kn})^k < exact false-positive rate for k >= 2 (exact Fractions, m < 41, n <= 10, k <= 6); equality of the last two for k = 1
OK   e^{-kn/m} > (1 - 1/m)^{kn}: the approximation p overestimates the fraction of 0-bits
OK   the false-positive rate increases with n and decreases with m (exact, k = 2, 4)
OK   ln p ln(1-p) is maximized at p = 1/2 (grid of 10^4 points)
OK   for m/n = c the estimate (1 - e^{-k/c})^k is minimized at k = c ln 2, where it equals (1/2)^{c ln 2} (c = 4, 8, 10, 16, 32)
OK   (1/2)^{ln 2} = e^{-(ln 2)^2} = 0.61850...  [0.618503]
OK   10n bits and 7 hash functions: estimated false-positive rate 0.82% (< 1%)  [0.00819]
OK   32n bits and 22 hash functions: 2.10e-7, 'just over 2 * 10^-7'  [2.104e-07]
OK   delta = 1%: the best integer k at m/n = 9.585 is 7
OK   delta = 1%: m/n = lg(100)/ln 2 = 9.585 and k = ceil(lg 100) = 7 give an estimate of 1.004% -- slightly ABOVE delta; no integer k reaches 1% at that size  [0.010039]
OK   the sizing rule m = ceil(n lg(1/delta)/ln 2), k = ceil(lg(1/delta)) gives exactly delta when lg(1/delta) is an integer and up to 3% more otherwise (ratio estimate/delta)  [0.5: 1.0000; 0.25: 1.0000; 0.1: 1.0260; 0.05: 1.0206; 0.01: 1.0039; 0.001: 1.0000; 0.0001: 1.0079; 1e-06: 1.0000]
OK   m = n lg(1/delta)/ln 2 = 1.44 n lg(1/delta) bits
OK   exact false-positive rate, n = 1000, m = 10000, k = 7: 0.82%, just above the estimate  [0.008201 vs 0.008194]
OK   exact false-positive rate, n = 1000, m = 32000, k = 22: 2.1e-7, just above the estimate  [2.1093e-07 vs 2.1042e-07]
OK   simulation: 20 filters with n = 1000, m = 10000, k = 7 and 20000 queries each: false positives 0.82% +- 0.05%  [0.00837]
OK   bits per key c = 4, 8, 10, 16: best integer k = 3, 6, 7, 11 with estimated rates 14.7%, 2.16%, 0.82%, 0.046%  [{4: (3, '14.6892%'), 8: (6, '2.1577%'), 10: (7, '0.8194%'), 16: (11, '0.0459%')}]
== §6.4 The count-min sketch
OK   figure example: stream a b a c a d b a (N = 8) into 3 rows of 5 counters gives rows [4,0,3,1,0], [0,3,0,5,0], [2,4,0,0,2]
OK   figure example: estimates a 4, b 2, c 1 (exact) and d = min(3, 5, 2) = 2 > f_d = 1
OK   CMEstimate never underestimates, and Count[i, h_i(x)] = f_x + X_{i,x} exactly (200 random sketches)
OK   one row, over every salt of the universal family ((ax+b) mod 31) mod w: E[X_{i,x}] <= N/w and Pr[X_{i,x} > eps N] <= E[X]/(eps N) <= 1/(w eps) (exact)
OK   the note's strict claim '< delta' holds: Pr[X_{i,x} > eps N] is 0 or strictly below E[X]/(eps N) (same exact computation), so the product over d rows is strictly below (1/(w eps))^d <= delta
OK   eps = 0.01, delta = 0.001: w = ceil(e/eps) = 272, d = ceil(ln(1/delta)) = 7, 1904 counters, and (1/(w eps))^d <= delta
OK   eps = 0.05, delta = 0.05: w = ceil(e/eps) = 55, d = ceil(ln(1/delta)) = 3, 165 counters, and (1/(w eps))^d <= delta
OK   eps = 0.1, delta = 0.01: w = ceil(e/eps) = 28, d = ceil(ln(1/delta)) = 5, 140 counters, and (1/(w eps))^d <= delta
OK   simulation, eps = delta = 0.05 (w = 55, d = 3), Zipf streams of 3000 items, 60 sketches x 500 queries: the fraction of estimates above f_x + eps N is at most delta  [4/30000 = 0.0001; largest overestimate 0.068 N]
